---
scope: src/tools
intent: >
  The tools layer is where each AIDE lifecycle operation is exposed to the MCP
  runtime as a thin handler. Every tool in this folder has one job: validate
  its input, delegate all real work to the lib layer, and shape the result
  into the text response the agent will read. The tool file's substance is
  its description — the handler itself should be boring.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Each AIDE lifecycle operation is exposed as exactly one tool, and the
      tool's substance lives in its description, not its handling logic.
    - Real work — filesystem scanning, tree construction, classification,
      scaffold generation, validation reasoning — happens in the lib layer.
      The tools layer only receives a validated request, calls lib, and
      formats the result.
    - Input validation happens at the tools layer boundary so the agent gets
      a readable message on bad input rather than a downstream crash.
    - Errors return as text in the MCP response, never as thrown exceptions
      that would surface to the agent as an opaque MCP error.
    - Output is shaped for an agent to read, not a program to parse —
      human-readable text with enough structure that the agent can pattern
      match on sections but no JSON-as-string or other machine formats.
  undesired:
    - A tool that touches the filesystem directly instead of asking lib to do
      the work. A tool reaching past its layer is the first sign the layering
      has broken.
    - A tool reaching into another tool's territory. Tools compose from lib,
      never from each other — if two tools need the same logic, that logic
      becomes a lib capability.
    - A tool that throws on bad input, empty results, or missing paths.
      Every failure mode must become a readable text response so the agent
      gets actionable feedback instead of a cryptic MCP error.
    - Tool descriptions that describe the input schema instead of teaching
      the concept. The schema is machine-readable; the description is the
      only place the convention gets taught.
---

## Context

The tools layer is the seam where the MCP protocol meets the AIDE convention.
MCP gives this layer one guaranteed capability — tool descriptions are
presented to the model verbatim — and one hard constraint — handlers run
behind a protocol that turns any thrown error into an opaque failure message
the agent can't reason about. Every rule in this spec exists to protect that
capability and work around that constraint.

## Strategy

**Handlers stay thin so descriptions can stay thick.** The teaching surface
of this layer is the set of tool descriptions the agent receives at
connection time — that is where AIDE conventions get taught. Pushing real
work down into lib keeps the tool-level reading experience dominated by the
description, not by implementation noise. If a tool's handling logic grows
past "validate, delegate, format," the new complexity belongs one layer
below.

**Validate input at the boundary, before anything else runs.** The tools
layer is the first place where an agent's request becomes structured data,
so it is also the only place that can turn a malformed request into a
readable message instead of a crash. Validation lives right next to the
operation it guards so the accepted shape and the accepted behavior cannot
drift apart.

**Never throw on expected failures.** Missing paths, empty result sets,
bad inputs, and filesystem-access errors are all return values, not
exceptions. The agent must see a message like "Path not found: foo/bar"
and be able to adjust, not an opaque "MCP tool error" with no context. The
only exceptions that should propagate are genuine programming bugs the
agent can do nothing with.

**Compose from lib, never from siblings.** Tools do not reach into each
other: the discover tool does not reach into the read tool; the validate
tool does not reach into the scaffold tool. Shared logic lives in lib. This keeps each tool's failure modes
independent and prevents a change in one tool from silently breaking
another through the back door.

**Shape output for agents, not for programs.** An MCP response is text the
model reads. Format it with headers and lists and plain English, not as
JSON-stringified objects. The agent pattern-matches on text better than it
parses serialized data, and the tokens are cheaper to read than to unpack.

## Good examples

When the agent calls `aide_validate` with no arguments on a clean project,
the response the agent sees is:

> No issues found.

When the agent calls `aide_validate` on a project with problems, the
response is one line per issue in a consistent pattern:

> [naming-conflict] src/service/foo — folder contains both `.aide` and `intent.aide`
> [orphaned-research] src/service/bar — `research.aide` has no intent spec alongside
> [missing-spec] src/service/baz — orchestrator imports 4 helpers but has no `.aide`

Each line carries a bracketed issue type the agent can pattern-match on,
followed by the offending path and a human-readable explanation. No
envelope, no serialized structure — the text is the response.

When the agent calls a tool with a path that doesn't exist, the response is:

> Path not found: src/nonexistent

The agent sees the actual problem and can retry with a corrected argument.
No error is thrown; the MCP response is just this text.

A well-written tool description — the thing an agent sees when MCP lists
available tools — teaches the convention:

> aide_scaffold — creates a new `.aide` spec (or a paired `intent.aide` +
> `research.aide`) next to an orchestrator. Defaults to a single `.aide`
> file. Use `intent.aide` + `research.aide` only when research is
> inseparable from the code; prefer pushing research to the brain.

This description explains *why* the tool exists, not just *what* it accepts.

## Bad examples

A tool response that dumps a serialized structure the agent has to parse:

> {"warnings":[{"type":"naming-conflict","path":"src/service/foo","message":"both .aide and intent.aide"}],"summary":{"total":1}}

The agent now has to reason about schema keys instead of reading a
sentence. Every token in that envelope is a token the description could
have used for teaching.

A tool response that surfaces an internal failure as an MCP error instead
of a text response: the agent receives "unexpected failure" with no context
and cannot tell whether the project has no specs yet, the path is wrong,
or the server itself is broken. All three failure modes look identical
from the other side of the protocol.

A tool description that parrots the input schema:

> aide_scaffold(directory, type): creates a file. `type` is one of
> "intent", "research", "both", "todo".

This teaches nothing. The agent already has the parameter names from the
schema, and it still has no idea when to use `intent` versus `both`, or
that the `.aide`-vs-`intent.aide` distinction exists at all. The
description is the only place that lesson can be taught, and this
description wastes it.

A tool response that mixes domain output with stack traces or internal
paths from the server process. The agent sees noise it can't act on, and
the meaningful signal — the one line that says what's actually wrong —
gets buried in context that belongs in server logs, not in the response.
